\chapter{Tokens, comments, and source files}
MuLL source is a byte string. Keywords and identifiers use ASCII; quoted
strings can carry arbitrary bytes, including UTF-8. An identifier begins with
a letter or underscore and continues with letters, digits, or underscores.
Names are case-sensitive: \api{Printf} and \api{printf} are different bindings.

\begin{mullcode}
# A hash starts a comment through the end of the line.
val answer_2 <- 40 + 2; // This comment form works too.
G:Assert(answer_2 == 42);
\end{mullcode}

Whitespace separates tokens but does not terminate statements. This permits a
long import or function call to span several lines. Comments end at a newline
or EOF. Block comments are not part of the grammar.

\section{Literals and reserved words}
Numbers use decimal notation, optionally with a decimal point and exponent:
\api{12}, \api{12.5}, \api{1e3}, and \api{2.5e-2}. A minus sign is an
operator rather than part of the numeric token. Strings use double quotes.
The literals \api{true}, \api{false}, and \api{nil} have dedicated meanings.

The reserved words are \api{val}, \api{var}, \api{ref}, \api{fn}, \api{if},
\api{else}, \api{while}, \api{return}, \api{break}, \api{and}, \api{or},
\api{not}, and the three literal names. Record keys that collide with a keyword
can be quoted and selected with string indexing.

Each token retains a one-based line and byte column. The entire input is parsed
before execution begins, so a syntax error prevents earlier statements in the
same run from producing effects.
